00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.880
Good morning John!

00:00:01.040 --> 00:00:03.040
It is that magical time of year

00:00:03.040 --> 00:00:07.340
between the time when we give gifts and the time when we celebrate the new year.

00:00:07.340 --> 00:00:10.440
It's magical in that I don't have anything that I'm supposed to be doing.

00:00:10.440 --> 00:00:13.660
I don't know how! And I kinda just wanna go back to work.

00:00:13.660 --> 00:00:16.600
Which, maybe I'm a workaholic or maybe I just have a really great job.

00:00:16.600 --> 00:00:21.340
Yeah, this is the one time of the year when I get to decide for myself what I wanna do.

00:00:21.340 --> 00:00:22.420
So that's sledding

00:00:22.420 --> 00:00:23.340
[music] [Hank's voice] huaaaagh!

00:00:23.340 --> 00:00:25.020
[music continues]

00:00:25.020 --> 00:00:26.660
[Hank] Aaaaau this was a bad idea!

00:00:26.660 --> 00:00:30.220
[Hank, offscreen] there's a bush. Right towards the bush!

00:00:30.220 --> 00:00:31.480
[music]

00:00:31.480 --> 00:00:33.640
And answering science questions on Tumblr.

00:00:33.640 --> 00:00:36.820
Also playing video games, eating fried egg sandwiches, cuddling,

00:00:36.820 --> 00:00:38.640
But mostly answering science questions on Tumblr.

00:00:38.640 --> 00:00:43.680
It turns out that it is just one of my favorite things in the world to do and if you haven't seen my Tumblr asks,

00:00:43.680 --> 00:00:49.020
Here are some of the fascinating things about the world that I have learned due to people asking me questions on Tumblr.

00:00:49.020 --> 00:00:50.220
There is a kidney.

00:00:50.660 --> 00:00:52.940
that has been in three different people.

00:00:52.940 --> 00:00:55.600
The person who donated it, she donated it to her brother,

00:00:55.600 --> 00:00:58.640
and then he couldn't use it, it didn't work, for whatever reason

00:00:58.640 --> 00:01:02.100
so they took it out and they were like, "So, this is yours now,

00:01:02.100 --> 00:01:05.020
you have rights over this thing,

00:01:05.020 --> 00:01:07.820
what should we-- can we put it in another person or should we throw it away?"

00:01:07.820 --> 00:01:12.700
and he was like, "Put it in another per--" but I think it's fascinating that bec-- like, the MOMENT it entered his body cavity,

00:01:12.700 --> 00:01:14.880
it was his. And he got to decide.

00:01:14.880 --> 00:01:18.020
Not his sister, who was the original owner of the kidney,

00:01:18.020 --> 00:01:22.100
And not the medical establishment, who had the kidney and put it inside of him,

00:01:22.100 --> 00:01:23.520
But him, the guy.

00:01:23.520 --> 00:01:25.260
And he was like, "Yeah give it to somebody else." And they did.

00:01:25.260 --> 00:01:27.100
So that kidney has been in three different people.

00:01:27.100 --> 00:01:30.840
Sperm whales undergo massive changes in pressure.

00:01:30.840 --> 00:01:34.880
like, if you take deep sea fish up to not-deep-sea locations, they explode.

00:01:34.880 --> 00:01:38.740
And if you take fish from the top of the ocean and bring them to the bottom, then they get crushed.

00:01:38.740 --> 00:01:43.460
But sperm whales, they go from like the top of the ocean all the way down to like 2,000 feet below the ocean.

00:01:43.460 --> 00:01:46.400
Which is 200 atmospheres different in pressure.

00:01:46.400 --> 00:01:50.420
And, they're mammals, so they have lungs which are filled with air, which can cause all kinds of problems

00:01:50.420 --> 00:01:52.120
with nitrogen getting into your-- it's crazy!

00:01:52.120 --> 00:01:52.820
How do they do this?!

00:01:52.820 --> 00:01:56.520
When they dive, they actually collapse their own lungs,

00:01:57.100 --> 00:01:58.300
So that that's not a problem.

00:01:58.420 --> 00:02:01.980
And then all of the oxygen they need is stored in their blood, in their hemoglobin,

00:02:01.980 --> 00:02:05.820
and in special oxygen-storage proteins in their muscles.

00:02:05.880 --> 00:02:08.680
The blink of an eye. Sounds like it's fast.

00:02:08.680 --> 00:02:11.600
and it does not take very much time, only about 300 milliseconds,

00:02:11.600 --> 00:02:13.960
but in those 300 milliseconds, it only travels about 1 inch,

00:02:13.960 --> 00:02:17.420
So it's actually very slow, it's like, a fifth of a mile per hour,

00:02:17.480 --> 00:02:18.520
Slow blinking.

00:02:18.520 --> 00:02:19.760
We have created a drug

00:02:20.340 --> 00:02:22.820
which prevents hair from going gray.

00:02:22.820 --> 00:02:24.940
It actually reverses the graying process.

00:02:24.940 --> 00:02:25.440
But!

00:02:25.460 --> 00:02:28.040
It causes diarrhea and nausea and occasionally death.

00:02:28.040 --> 00:02:29.120
So that's not good.

00:02:29.120 --> 00:02:32.080
But it also is good for treating leukemia.

00:02:32.080 --> 00:02:34.300
and it costs about 90,000 dollars a year.

00:02:34.300 --> 00:02:35.340
So. Hair dye!

00:02:35.340 --> 00:02:38.480
We do not know, if the core of Jupiter is a liquid or a solid.

00:02:38.480 --> 00:02:41.860
It is very hot, but also under tremendous amounts of pressure.

00:02:41.860 --> 00:02:45.700
In fact, if Jupiter was just 10 times bigger, there would be so much pressure,

00:02:45.700 --> 00:02:48.960
that there would be sustainable fusion reactions going on inside of it

00:02:48.960 --> 00:02:51.660
and we would live in a binary star system.

00:02:51.680 --> 00:02:54.600
My all-time favorite question though, that has been asked of me on Tumblr is:

00:02:54.600 --> 00:02:59.920
"If I weigh 99 pounds and I eat 1 pound of nachos, does that mean I am 1% nacho?"

00:02:59.920 --> 00:03:04.840
This question turns out to be so complicated that I cannot answer it in this video.

00:03:04.840 --> 00:03:07.440
We may do an entire episode of SciShow on it.

00:03:07.520 --> 00:03:08.400
It's fascinating.

00:03:08.400 --> 00:03:11.000
I hope you enjoyed these fascinating science facts that I have learned,

00:03:11.000 --> 00:03:15.960
I love the world, and I love weird, exciting interesting questions about the world,

00:03:15.960 --> 00:03:18.580
So thank you to people on Tumblr for doing this, and yes,

00:03:18.580 --> 00:03:22.940
I do know that now I'm going to get like, way more asks than I can answer.

00:03:22.940 --> 00:03:26.520
So, sorry, I probably won't be able to answer your asks.

00:03:26.520 --> 00:03:28.580
But I am going on Reddit right now to do an AMA.

00:03:28.580 --> 00:03:30.700
If you would like to join me, you can ask me anything.

00:03:30.700 --> 00:03:34.700
I just feel like answer-y question-y today, though my wrist is already starting to hurt.

00:03:34.700 --> 00:03:38.280
From the typing. So. Not from the video games, I promise.

